WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.400
Cassian Andor lives before the battle of Yavin&nbsp;
in the year BBY 5. But he doesn't know that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.600
because the pivotal 'Star Wars' event this&nbsp;
system of time is based on is still years away.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.340
Time is a fleeting concept, one that becomes&nbsp;
even more amorphous when considering how it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.140
function across an entire galaxy. According to&nbsp;
NASA, our Milky Way galaxy is thought to contain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.600
somewhere between 100 to 400 billion stars, and&nbsp;
measures 100,000 light years in length. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.560
means that if you were so inclined to travel from&nbsp;
one edge of our galaxy to another, it would take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.000
100,000 years of traveling at the speed of light&nbsp;
to reach the other side. Needless to say, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.080
vastness of space can be something of a daunting&nbsp;
prospect regarding the conventions of time.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.860
"Andor" is Disney's latest romp through&nbsp;
its flagship "Star Wars" universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.540
chronicling how Cassian Andor becomes the&nbsp;
die-hard rebel leader featured in "Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.040
Rogue One." Time-wise, "Andor" is a prequel&nbsp;
television series that takes place before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.480
original "Star Wars" trilogy but after the "Star&nbsp;
Wars" prequel trilogy, placing its story smack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.740
dab in the midst of a fermenting rebellion. More&nbsp;
specifically, the very first moments of "Andor"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:03.180
see Cassian walking into a city on Morlana One,&nbsp;
and the show mentions that this is taking place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:09.180
during "BBY 5." Obviously, that denotes a&nbsp;
year. However, what exactly does BBY 5 mean?

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.160
In the real world, our calendar categorizes&nbsp;
years as either the secular "BCE" and "CE,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.480
referring to the "Common Era," or "BC"&nbsp;
or "AD," which is a system heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.220
influenced by Christianity. B.C. stands&nbsp;
for "before Christ," while A.D. is Latin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.120
for "anno domini," which translates&nbsp;
to "in the year of the lord." So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.320
what does "BBY" mean in "Star Wars?" Could&nbsp;
it mean "before Baby Yoda," for example?

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.580
Unfortunately, no, Grogu is not part of&nbsp;
the Star Wars dating system. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.740
BBY does reference an incredibly historic moment&nbsp;
in the "Star Wars" universe: The Battle of Yavin.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.180
For the non-hardcore "Star Wars" fans, Yavin&nbsp;
IV is the moon that hosts the secretive base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.140
of the Rebel Alliance in the first "Star&nbsp;
Wars" movie. The Battle of Yavin refers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.780
to the skirmish that results in the&nbsp;
destruction of the first Death Star.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.760
"Lock S-Foils in attack position."

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.700
This means that "Andor" takes place five years&nbsp;
before this momentous event. This is a victory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.200
that Cassian Andor is partially responsible&nbsp;
for. In "Rogue One" he aids in the theft of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.640
the original Death Star plans. By stealing these&nbsp;
plans, Cassian and the rest of his team provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.720
the Rebel Alliance with the means to destroy the&nbsp;
Death Star, which otherwise would have become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.340
yet another symbol of Imperial oppression. From&nbsp;
that point on, years in the "Star Wars" universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:26.640
are denoted as "Before the Battle of Yavin," aka&nbsp;
BBY, or "After the Battle of Yavin," aka "ABY."

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.180
Cassian Andor and the rest of the characters&nbsp;
featured throughout "Andor," of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.980
do not know they exist in the year BBY 5, given&nbsp;
that the Battle of Yavin has yet to occur.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.720
According to books like 2015's "Star Wars:&nbsp;
Absolutely Everything You Need to Know," galactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.160
authorities at one point considered year zero to&nbsp;
be the end of the Old Republic era featured in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.220
for example, the acclaimed "Star Wars"&nbsp;
video game "Knights of the Old Republic."

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.000
An interesting note is that the way&nbsp;
time passes on what was once the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.020
galactic capital Coruscant is akin to&nbsp;
how time passes on Earth, meaning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.940
a standard year in the "Star Wars" universe&nbsp;
similarly consists of 365 days of 24 hours.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.440
The Old Republic era, meanwhile,&nbsp;
ends at around BBY 1000. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.820
if Cassian indeed uses the system that considers&nbsp;
the end of the Old Republic as year zero during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.600
the events of "Andor," he likely understands&nbsp;
himself to exist somewhere around the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:18.600
995. Sometime after the Battle of Yavin, the&nbsp;
BBY/ABY system is introduced to the galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.040
eventually granting its inhabitants the same&nbsp;
timekeeping device frequently shown to viewers.

